---
title: "Calculated Columns"
enterprise: true
---

Calculated Columns let your end users add read-only values to the grid without storing those values in row data.

## Enabling Calculated Columns

Calculated columns are enabled by setting `calculatedColumns: true`, and setting `calculatedExpression` on a column definition. In source code, bracket references such as `[revenue]` resolve to the same-row value from the column with that `colId`.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: true,
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'revenue' },
        { field: 'cost' },
        {
            colId: 'profit',
            calculatedExpression: '[revenue] - [cost]',
            cellDataType: 'number',
            sortable: true,
            filter: true,
        },
    ],
};
```

{% note %}
Application-declared calculated columns must define a `colId` explicitly.
{% /note %}

{% gridExampleRunner title="Calculated Columns" name="calculated-columns" /%}

Register [Cell Data Types](./cell-data-types/) to give users formatting options to pick from when they create a column. The example above provides a `currency` type — see [dataTypes](#datatypes).

Calculated expressions do not need a leading equals sign (`=`). The value inside brackets must match a column `colId`, which defaults to the `field` when no explicit `colId` is provided.

Calculated Columns are supported with all row models.

For Server-Side, Infinite and Viewport Row Models, sorting and filtering are datasource or server responsibilities. The grid displays calculated values for loaded rows, but does not client-sort or client-filter remote datasets by calculated results.

Calculated cell values cannot be changed through cell editing, paste, fill handle or delete operations. Users can still change a calculated column's title, type and expression through its dialog. To prevent these dialog edits and removal through the grid's menus, see [Read-Only Calculated Columns](#read-only-calculated-columns).

## Row Groups and Tree Data

Calculated columns are full columns, so [Row Grouping](./grouping/), [Tree Data](./tree-data/), [Sorting](./row-sorting/) and [Filtering](./filtering/), [Text Formatting](./value-formatters/) and [Cell Components](./component-cell-renderer/) apply to them the same way as any other column.

A calculated column evaluates on rows that have their own data: leaf rows, and Tree Data parents that carry data. It stays blank on rows without data — row group rows, group footers, the grand-total row, and Tree Data filler nodes.

To show a value on group rows, give the calculated column an `aggFunc`. Each leaf evaluates the expression, and the group aggregates those per-leaf results with the chosen function — the same as a column with a `valueGetter`. For example `{ calculatedExpression: '[revenue] - [cost]', aggFunc: 'sum' }` shows the total of its rows' profits on a group. A ratio has no meaningful aggregation, so a column like `[profit] / [revenue]` is left without an `aggFunc` and stays blank on group rows.

Under [Pivoting](./pivoting/), a calculated column with an `aggFunc` is a value column: it evaluates per leaf and its results aggregate into the pivot result columns, the same as a column with a `valueGetter`. Without an `aggFunc` it is not a value column, so it has no pivot result column and is absent from the cross-tab, like any other non-value column.

A calculated column can also be a pivot column (`pivot: true`): its per-row result becomes the pivot key, so the grid creates a result column for each distinct calculated value — the same as pivoting on a column with a `valueGetter`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Row Groups with Calculated Columns" name="calculated-columns-row-groups" /%}

## References with Column Groups

Register the Column Menu so users can add a calculated column from the header menu with **Add Calculated Column**. The header menu on a calculated column shows **Edit Calculated Column** to change its title, type and expression, and **Remove Calculated Column** to remove it. Right-clicking cells in a calculated column also shows **Remove Calculated Column**.

The dialog shows references by header name, for example `[Revenue]`. When headers are duplicated under groups, it uses the shortest unique group path, such as `[2025 Q4]` and `[2026 Q4]`. It translates these display references back to `colId` references before updating the column definition.

The expression editor highlights references, functions and operators. In deferred edit dialogs, validation runs as you edit and on Apply, not when the dialog opens. Built-in errors are underlined, explained in a tooltip and announced to assistive technology. Live mode shows reference-restriction and custom validation errors, but does not automatically show syntax errors in the editor; invalid expressions still produce formula errors in grid cells.

In dialog references, a literal `]` in a header name is escaped as `]]`. For example, the header `Revenue [USD]` appears as `[Revenue [USD]]]`. The Columns picker inserts the escaped reference automatically; stored `colId` references are unchanged.

If two columns share the same header _and_ the same group hierarchy, the dialog falls back to appending the `colId`, for example `[2025 Q4 (revenue-q4)]`. This keeps the reference stable when columns are reordered. Give your columns distinct headers or group hierarchies to avoid this.

The following example has duplicate `Q1`, `Q2`, `Q3` and `Q4` headers under `2025` and `2026` column groups. The year groups start collapsed and show a calculated `Total` column; expanding a group reveals the quarter columns. The dialog shows grouped references such as `[2025 Q4]` and `[2026 Q4]`, while the source code stores stable `colId` references such as `[q4_2025]` and `[q4_2026]`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Column Groups with Duplicate Headers" name="calculated-columns-column-groups" /%}

The dialog manages calculated columns inside the grid without mutating the `columnDefs` array supplied by the application. To persist user-created calculated columns, read the complete current definitions from `api.getColumnDefs()`, or use [Grid State](./grid-state/), which captures them and recreates them on restore.

Grid State also captures user edits to, and removals of, calculated columns declared in `columnDefs`, and re-applies them over the declared definitions on restore — so a stored state takes precedence over a changed declaration.

Use Grid State to preserve dialog validation errors as well. `api.getColumnDefs()` contains the column configuration, not validation errors; restoring definitions alone treats the expressions as programmatic definitions.

## Read-Only Calculated Columns

Set `calculatedColumnReadOnly: true` to prevent users from changing a column's title, type or expression in the dialog. Users can choose **View Calculated Column** to inspect its definition. **Remove Calculated Column** is disabled in the column and cell context menus.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: true,
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'revenue' },
        { field: 'cost' },
        {
            colId: 'grossMargin',
            calculatedExpression: '([revenue] - [cost]) / [revenue]',
            cellDataType: 'number',
            calculatedColumnReadOnly: true,
        },
    ],
};
```

The example keeps **Gross Margin** read-only, while **Profit** and user-created calculated columns remain editable.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Read-Only Calculated Columns" name="calculated-columns-read-only" /%}

{% note %}
This setting restricts UI changes only: updating `columnDefs` and restoring [Grid State](./grid-state/) can still change or remove the column. Columns created through the dialog remain editable, even when `defaultColDef.calculatedColumnReadOnly` is `true`.
{% /note %}

### Hiding Menu Items

The calculated column actions are built-in menu items. To stop users adding, editing or removing calculated columns from the menus, filter them out of the default items with [getColumnMenuItems](./column-menu/#customising-the-menu-items) and [getContextMenuItems](./context-menu/#configuring-the-context-menu):

- `calculatedColumn`: **Add Calculated Column**, in the column menu.
- `editCalculatedColumn`: **Edit Calculated Column**, or **View Calculated Column** when the column is read-only, in the column menu.
- `removeCalculatedColumn`: **Remove Calculated Column**, in the column menu and the cell context menu.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: true,
    getColumnMenuItems: ({ defaultItems }) =>
        defaultItems.filter(
            item => item !== 'editCalculatedColumn' && item !== 'removeCalculatedColumn'
        ),
    getContextMenuItems: ({ defaultItems }) =>
        defaultItems?.filter(item => item !== 'removeCalculatedColumn'),
};
```

As with [read-only columns](#read-only-calculated-columns), this only removes the menu entries. Updating `columnDefs` and restoring [Grid State](./grid-state/) can still change or remove the column.

## Dialog Options

The Calculated Column dialog can be configured with the `calculatedColumns` grid option. Set `calculatedColumns: true` to enable the feature with default dialog options, or provide an options object to enable the feature and customise the dialog.

While a column's dialog is open, the grid highlights the column's header and cells. Customise the highlight colour with the `--ag-calculated-column-highlight-color` CSS variable.

### dataTypes

Use `dataTypes` to control which cell data types appear in the dialog type dropdown. The list can include built-in cell data types and custom types registered with `dataTypeDefinitions`:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    dataTypeDefinitions: {
        currency: {
            extendsDataType: 'number',
            baseDataType: 'number',
            valueFormatter: params =>
                params.value == null ? '' : `$${params.value.toLocaleString()}`,
        },
    },
    calculatedColumns: {
        dataTypes: ['currency', 'number', 'boolean'],
    },
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'revenue', cellDataType: 'currency' },
        { field: 'cost', cellDataType: 'currency' },
        {
            colId: 'profit',
            headerName: 'Profit',
            calculatedExpression: '[revenue] - [cost]',
            cellDataType: 'currency',
        },
    ],
};
```

Applying the type to your own columns as well means a user who picks **Currency** in the dialog gets the same formatting as the columns you declared. A `valueFormatter` on a column definition cannot be selected in the dialog, so it is not available to users.

Built-in types use the grid's locale text, while custom type names are converted to readable labels in the dialog. If a selected value does not match a registered [Cell Data Type](./cell-data-types/), the grid's normal `cellDataType` validation applies when the calculated column is created.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Dialog Data Types" name="calculated-columns-dialog-data-types" /%}

### expressionPickers

Use `expressionPickers` to control which expression pickers appear:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        expressionPickers: ['columns'],
    },
};
```

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="CalculatedColumnExpressionPicker" config={"asCode":true} /%}

This only controls the expression picker buttons. Use an empty array or `null` to hide all picker buttons. Inline autocomplete while typing in the expression editor remains available.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Dialog Expression Pickers" name="calculated-columns-dialog-helper-lists" /%}

### isColumnReferenceable

Use `isColumnReferenceable` to control which source columns can be referenced in the dialog. Return `false` to hide a column from the Columns picker and inline autocomplete, and reject manually entered references to it:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        isColumnReferenceable: ({ colDef, calculatedColumn }) =>
            colDef.field !== 'salary' || calculatedColumn?.getColId() === 'bonus',
    },
};
```

The callback runs when the dialog opens. It receives the source `column` and `colDef`, the `calculatedColumn` being edited, and the usual `api` and `context` parameters. `calculatedColumn` is `null` when adding a new column in either apply mode. Changes to application state take effect the next time the dialog opens; they do not update an already-open picker.

An existing expression that was not blocked by the dialog can be kept unchanged even if it references a restricted column. Users can apply it in deferred mode or change its title and type in either mode. Once the expression text differs from the text displayed when the dialog opened, restricted references are blocked, including edits to casing, whitespace or arithmetic. Restoring the original permitted expression text clears the restriction error.

In deferred mode, a blocked reference disables Apply. In live mode, the full expression is saved with its restriction error, and cells display `#REF!` instead of evaluating it. Closing and reopening the dialog preserves the full expression and error so the user can correct it. Changing the title or type does not clear the restriction. Grid State also preserves the blocked status.

Programmatic `calculatedExpression` values remain unrestricted. Restoring state preserves whether an expression was permitted or blocked; it does not apply new restrictions to previously permitted expressions. This is an editor restriction, not a data-security boundary.

Visibility does not affect eligibility: hidden columns can be offered, and visible columns can be restricted. The picker uses primary columns in and out of pivot mode, excludes generated service columns, and keeps reference names stable when other columns are restricted.

The following example permits Salary references when editing Bonus but excludes them when editing Sales Target or adding a new calculated column.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Dialog Reference Restrictions" name="calculated-columns-reference-restrictions" /%}

### getValidationErrors

Use `getValidationErrors` to customise expression validation in either apply mode, including for invalid expressions. The callback receives built-in messages in `internalErrors`. Return the messages to show, or `null` or an empty array to accept the expression. Return `internalErrors` to keep the built-in validation.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        applyMode: 'deferred',
        getValidationErrors: ({ referencedColumns, internalErrors }) =>
            referencedColumns.some(column => column.getColId() === 'salary')
                ? ['Salary cannot be used in this report.']
                : internalErrors,
    },
};
```

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="CalculatedColumnValidationParams" /%}

In the example, edit **Sales Bonus** and enter `[Salary]` to see the validation messages and disabled Apply button.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Custom Dialog Validation" name="calculated-columns-custom-validation" /%}

{% note %}
Validation applies only in the dialog; programmatic definitions are unaffected. Errors block Apply in deferred mode. In live mode, rejected edits are saved but display `#ERROR!` until corrected. Title and type edits also revalidate unchanged expressions. Restrictions set by `isColumnReferenceable` remain enforced.
{% /note %}

### suppressColumnHighlighting

Use `suppressColumnHighlighting` to disable the highlight while the dialog is open:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    theme: myTheme,
    calculatedColumns: {
        suppressColumnHighlighting: true,
    },
};
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Suppress Dialog Column Highlighting" name="calculated-columns-dialog-highlighting" /%}

### applyMode

By default, dialog edits apply to the column immediately: **Add Calculated Column** creates the column and opens the dialog over it, and title, type and expression changes update the column as you type. Closing the dialog keeps the latest state; remove the column with **Remove Calculated Column**. An empty expression renders blank cells, and invalid or incomplete expressions render formula errors until fixed. Expression edits containing references excluded by `isColumnReferenceable` are saved but blocked from evaluation, preserving the full text for correction.

Set `applyMode: 'deferred'` to hold changes until you click **Apply** instead. The dialog shows Apply and Cancel buttons and validates edits before applying them:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: {
        applyMode: 'deferred',
    },
};
```

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="CalculatedColumnApplyMode" config={"asCode":true} /%}

{% gridExampleRunner title="Deferred Apply Mode" name="calculated-columns-apply-mode" /%}

## Advanced Calculated Columns

Calculated columns can use the same [Mathematical Operators](./formula-reference/#mathematical-operators) and [Provided Functions](./formula-reference/#provided-functions) as [formulas](./formulas/), and can reference other calculated columns in the same row. This allows chained derived values such as profit, margin and status.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    calculatedColumns: true,
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'revenue' },
        { field: 'cost' },
        {
            colId: 'profit',
            calculatedExpression: '[revenue] - [cost]',
            cellDataType: 'currency',
        },
        {
            colId: 'margin',
            calculatedExpression: '[profit] / [revenue]',
            cellDataType: 'percentage',
        },
        {
            colId: 'status',
            calculatedExpression: 'IF([margin] >= 0.25, "Healthy", "Review")',
            cellDataType: 'text',
        },
    ],
};
```

The `currency` and `percentage` types are registered with `dataTypeDefinitions`, as shown in [dataTypes](#datatypes).

{% gridExampleRunner title="Advanced Calculated Columns" name="calculated-columns-advanced" /%}

Header cells and grid cells for calculated columns include the `ag-calculated-column` CSS class; use it for custom styling.

A column added from the header menu appears after the column it was created from.

## API

Manage calculated columns through column definitions, like other columns. Read the current definitions with `api.getColumnDefs()` and write them back with `api.setGridOption('columnDefs', ...)` to add, edit or remove a calculated column. To find the calculated columns in the grid, filter `api.getColumns()` by `calculatedExpression`.

The dialog manages calculated columns inside the grid without mutating the `columnDefs` array supplied by the application. To persist user-created calculated columns, read the complete current definitions from `api.getColumnDefs()`.

The following example adds, edits and removes a Profit Margin column through these APIs, and logs the calculated columns currently in the grid:

{% gridExampleRunner title="Calculated Columns API" name="calculated-columns-api" /%}

Calculated Column events also use stored `colId` references in their expression payloads. For example, an event raised from the dialog still reports `[revenue]`, not `[Revenue]`.

